    "text": ": a crispy or soft corn or wheat tortilla that is folded or rolled and stuffed with a mixture (as of seasoned meat, cheese, and lettuce) Examples of taco in a Sentence Recent Examples on the Web Examples are automatically compiled from online sources to show current usage. Read More Opinions expressed in the examples do not represent those of Merriam-Webster or its editors. Send us feedback. Blackened Chicken can be served with rice, steamed vegetables, and pasta, but is also used for sandwiches, tacos, and salads. — Phoebe Evans, Southern Living, 13 Apr. 2026 And of course, there’s plenty of tacos. — Bud Kennedy, Fort Worth Star-Telegram, 13 Apr. 2026 So when might Idahoans enjoy tacos and margaritas in their shiny new grill and cantina? — Michael Deeds, Idaho Statesman, 13 Apr. 2026 Including the breakfast tacos at Lugya’h. — Bill Addison, Los Angeles Times, 11 Apr. 2026 See All Example Sentences for taco Word History Etymology Mexican Spanish First Known Use 1901, in the meaning defined above Time Traveler The first known use of taco was in 1901 Browse Nearby Words Cite this Entry “Taco.” Merriam-Webster.com Dictionary, Merriam-Webster, https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/taco. Accessed 16 Apr. 2026. More from Merriam-Webster on taco Subscribe to America's largest dictionary and get thousands more definitions and advanced search—ad free! Merriam-Webster unabridged More from Merriam-Webster Top Lookups Next refresh: 19",
